{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793383502,"nonce":"CTAJKudPwjYJ1cD7nrp9A1eILmo=","message":"Cloudflare check (read, not run). Durable Objects cannot host dynASB: RayDE's web/cloud DOs (AccountSession, WorkspaceSession) are control plane only; native/runner/README.md says 'Durable Objects do not run the VMM'. They call a trusted provisioning service (RUNNER service binding or RUNNER_URL, bearer RUNNER_SECRET): POST /workspaces/open {id, owner, repository, checkout} returns {url: wss session, token, project}, with idempotent reuse of the workspace id, GitHub checkout in the trusted plane (token never in the guest), private guest ports, bounded guests, a 409 recovery-required response that preserves used state, and checkpoint import/export plus doc.restore for recovery. So the question is whether dynASB can sit behind that contract on a KVM host. Gaps found by reading its API (src/api/server.rs, proto/control.proto): (1) no workspace concept: it deploys function code into runtime wrappers (Node, Python, Ruby, Lua, Elixir) and routes messages to ports; rayde-session is a static Rust binary with its own WebSocket protocol, so it needs a binary or container runtime and a stdio or WebSocket relay that dynASB does not have; (2) no persistent, fenced volumes: rootfs is built per deploy and state is in memory or Redis, while the plan needs a workspace volume and a document volume that survive VM loss; (3) no clone and checkout path, no idempotent open by workspace id, no recovery-required semantics; (4) auth is tenant JWT or API key, not the runner secret, and multi-tenant Raft, NATS and gRPC cluster machinery is heavier than a single-host workspace runner; (5) reachability: a Worker or DO reaches a private host only by fetch or an outbound WebSocket to a public or tunnelled address, as the NUC does over SSH forwarding today. Cloudflare Containers do not replace the KVM host: documented instance types top out at 4 vCPU, 12 GiB and 20 GB disk, and the docs do not state KVM or GPU support (a Cloudflare blog says GPUs are planned there). Conclusion for this issue: dynASB is a candidate libkrun and GPU engine behind the runner, not something that runs in a DO, and the adapter is the work: either a workspace backend inside dynASB that satisfies the contract, or a libkrun backend in rayde-runner that borrows dynASB's GPU and pool code.","files":null}]}